| A monster's damage-equipment attack told the player "the orcish bastard sword +1 protects its orcish bastard sword +1". `ETarget` and `EVictim` are not two fields; they are one event slot read through two macros (`inc/Events.h:216-217`), so the assignment that aimed the `EV_DAMAGE` rethrow at the item -- which is intended -- also erased the item's owner from the slot the message reads. Both halves broke, not only the wording: `VPrint` gives its first-person string to `e.EVictim`, so that string went to the ITEM, whose `Thing::__IPrint` is an empty virtual, and the character fell into the onlooker branch and got the garbled third-person line about his own sword. Moving the assignment below the save is the whole fix; the save path returns before the rethrow, so the rethrow still receives the item (`src/Fight.cpp`) | **Observed** -- seed 5, `tools/keys/dequ-save-message.keys`, two binaries differing in the position of one line. An orc warrior strikes a wizard-summoned brown pudding (A_DEQU 5d6 AD_DCAY at DC 19, live in `lib/program.i` as `60 for 5d6 57 (DC 19)`) six times with the save forced by an env-gated probe, `-DDEQU_PROBE`. Before: 6 of 6 strike screens read "The orcish knife +1 protects its orcish knife +1." and 0 of 6 carried the first-person line. After: 6 of 6 and 0 of 6 the other way round. The same count settles the routing, because in a one-player session `VPrint` cannot put both strings on one screen. Guarded by `tools/check_dequ_save_message.sh`, proved red on the pre-fix binary first | no | inc-473 |
| Fifty-eight printf-style format strings disagreed with their arguments, and nothing could see them: `Format` is a plain `vsprintf` (`src/Base.cpp:495`) and `Error` a plain `vsnprintf`, both varargs, and `build_macos.sh` compiled with `-w`. **Only 38 of the 58 are claimed here.** Those 38 misbehave on Win32 exactly as they do on macOS: 26 calls pass arguments the string never consumes; 3 pass FEWER arguments than it consumes, so `vsprintf` prints whatever the register file holds next (`Error("Strange Portal xval (%d).")` with no argument at `src/Feature.cpp:505`, `" %d (%d) resources\n"` with one at `src/Main.cpp:2154`, and a trailing colour `%c` with none at `src/Prayer.cpp:1350-1353`); 3 are invalid specifiers -- the `%l` at `src/Magic.cpp:575`, which also mis-typed the three arguments after it, and two `ASSERT`s whose asserted expression contained a `%` and so became a conversion specifier, because the macro pasted `#a` INTO the format string rather than passing it as an argument; and 6 pass an argument of a type wrong on any platform (a `const char*` printed with `%d` twice in `src/Debug.cpp`, and `narg+1` -- pointer arithmetic on an `int*` -- printed with `%d` in `lang/Grammar.acc`, where the sibling call five lines up correctly writes `(*narg)+1`). **The other 20 are NOT upstream's and are not claimed**: 14 are upstream code that is merely non-portable to LP64 (a `long`, `size_t`, `intptr_t` or `ptrdiff_t` that was 32 bits on Win32), and 6 are OURS, created by this port narrowing `int32`/`uint32`/`hObj` from `long` to `int` (`inc/Defines.h:48-63`) under format strings that still say `%ld`. The `ASSERT` fix is at the macro, not the two call sites, so every future `ASSERT` is immune. The `format(printf,1,2)` attribute stays on `Format` so the next one is a compile-time warning (`inc/Base.h`, `inc/Defines.h`) | **Observed** -- the compiler is the oracle: 58 `-Wformat` warnings before, 0 after, from `WARN_FLAGS="-Wno-everything -Wformat" EXTRA_CXXFLAGS=-DFMTAUDIT