| `Creature::gainFavour` declared its amount `int16` and `Character::gainFavour` redeclared it `int32`, so the derived function hid the base instead of overriding it and took a vtable slot of its own. Every call made through a `Creature*`, or from inside a `Creature::` method, reached the base body, which is empty, so five hard-coded favour awards wrote nothing: the favoured-skill award in `Creature::SkillCheck`, Khasrach's and Zurvash's devour awards in `Creature::DevourMonster`, and Semirath's trap-kill and once-per-trap awards in `Trap::TriggerTrap`. Upstream's, not the port's: a different parameter type is a different function under MSVC on Win32 too, and nothing here depends on a typedef, a width or a compiler of this port. `Transgress`, declared on the line above with matching signatures in both classes, is why transgressions always worked. Widened the base declaration to `int32`, with no cap on how often the favoured-skill award pays, on the owner's ruling of 2026-09-13 recorded in the bead (`inc/Creature.h`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_favour_awards.sh`, four sessions from frozen characters (`tools/fixtures/chars/lizardfolk-monk-seed1.char` on seed 5, `tools/fixtures/chars/kobold-rogue-seed1.char` on seed 3) under `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`. With `int16` the patron's favour on the character sheet did not move at any of seven events: Xavias 30000 -> 30000 on a Knowledge (Theology) success of 21 against DC 15; Khasrach 30002 -> 30002 and Zurvash 30000 -> 30000 on eating a tabaxi corpse; Semirath 30000 throughout two Handle Device checks, a trap kill, a second reset and a second kill on the same trap. With `int32` the same seven events paid +60, +20, +10, +370, +100, +250 and +50, each the amount the code computes from the printed roll and DC or from the creature's CR. Two controls held on both builds: Khasrach's own script paid +2 for the kill, and the once-per-trap branch paid +125 experience on the first kill only. The same mutation turns `tools/check_virtual_override.sh` red, and clang's `-Woverloaded-virtual` reported four hiding hits before the fix and three after | no | inc-rgzr |
| `Item::ReApply` cleared old grants with `RemoveStatiFrom(this)`, which is non-virtual and walks the ITEM's own rows -- it holds none keyed to itself -- never the wearer's. The isWield rethrow into `Magic::Grant` then added a second copy of every standing grant each time a worn item was re-applied: Magic Weapon cast on it, Storycraft raising its plus, a dispel ending. The lines are identical in upstream/master `src/Item.cpp`; plain base-code control flow, so it misbehaves the same on Win32 with the original typedefs and the upstream compiler. ReApply now removes, silently and only when it is about to re-add them, exactly the wearer's rows that rethrow re-creates (this item's handle and effect, `SS_ITEM`, no duration), which also keeps the removal out of `Magic::Dispel`'s own walk over the wearer. It holds current HP at maximum across the swap as `Character::UpdateTattoos` does, because each removal's `CalcValues` can otherwise throw a character at low HP into a death save (`src/Item.cpp`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_reapply_single_grant.sh`, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`. Unfixed engine: an orc wielding the Bloodspear read Will `+4 vs. spells`, `+8` with Magic Weapon on the spear and `+12` after it expired (seed 4); Storycraft raising a worn Periapt of Proof against Poisons from +1 to +2 read `+6 vs. poison` where `+4` is right (seed 1). Fixed: `+4`, `+4`, `+4` and `+4`. Proved red by four mutations: the unfixed engine turns S1 and S2 red; `cr->RemoveStatiFrom(this)` in place of the targeted removal deletes the Nine Lives Stealer's `SS_ENCH` activation row (S3); no HP guard moves a 7-HP bard to 9 HP through the death save (S5); and the removal outside the DISPELLED test loses the wearer's `ALIGNMENT` `SS_PERM` row during Dispel Magic (S4) | no | inc-7wmu |
| `Thing::StatiOff` (inc/Map.h) is virtual with the parameters `(Status s, bool elapsed = false)` and `Feature::StatiOff` (inc/Feature.h) declared only `(Status s)`, so the derived declaration hid the base instead of overriding it. The one call, in the stati-expiry macro in inc/Map.h, goes through a `Thing*`, so `Feature::StatiOff` (`src/Feature.cpp`) never ran, and a Feature made by a timed SUMMONED stati -- Wall of Doors' doors -- never vanished when the stati elapsed. Upstream's, not the port's: an arity difference is a different function under MSVC on Win32 with the upstream compiler too, and nothing here depends on a typedef, a width or a compiler of this port. Gave the derived declaration the base's parameter list (`inc/Feature.h`), matching the `StatiOn` declaration on the line above it | **Observed** -- `tools/check_summoned_feature_expiry.sh`, seed 1, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`, casting Wall of Doors as a first-level mage. The cast dump shows doors on the map; after 60 searches (about 17 game hours, beyond any duration) 13 doors remained with the one-parameter declaration and 0 with the fix, and the message log then carries "winks out of existence". The stale `Feature::StatiOff` line in `tools/virtual_override.baseline` was deleted with it | no | inc-rgzr |
| `Item::isMagic` (`inc/Item.h`) returns `eID || Plus` and is not virtual, while `Weapon::isMagic` and `Armour::isMagic` (`src/Item.cpp`) declare their own and also count an item quality -- `return Item::isMagic() || ItemLevel() > Item::ItemLevel()`, so that a `+0 corrosive long bow of speed` is not called mundane. A derived declaration against a non-virtual base opens a new slot instead of overriding, and every caller holds an `Item*`, so the derived bodies never ran and a weapon or armour carrying a quality with `Plus` 0 and no effect id counted as mundane. Upstream's, not the port's: a missing `virtual` is a missing `virtual` under MSVC on Win32 with the upstream compiler too, and no typedef of this port is involved. Made the base virtual (`inc/Item.h`), which is the whole change; nothing in `Weapon::isMagic`, `Armour::isMagic` or any `ItemLevel` function moved, and the known weapon/armour inconsistency in `ItemLevel` is out of scope. The player-visible effects, stated plainly: such weapons and armour now count as magical for identification and inscription, keep `IF_CURSED`/`IF_BLESSED` at generation (`src/Encounter.cpp:3506`), are used by planar monsters (`src/Monster.cpp:1040`, `:1146`), resist acid and hardness bypass (`src/Fight.cpp:4118`), and change god rules -- Kysul's and Maeve's gift acceptance (`lib/religion.irh:3377`, `lib/religion.irh:4307`) and Xavias' "damaging a magical item" transgression (`lib/religion.irh:5487`). Masterwork and materials do not count; only a quality or an effect id, exactly as `Weapon::isMagic` and `Armour::isMagic` compute. Owner's ruling 2026-10-05 | **Observed** -- `tools/check_quality_item_magic.sh`, seed 5, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`, the acid-blob hardness oracle at `src/Fight.cpp:4118` (`e2.ignoreHardness = (e2.saveDC <= 0 && !it->isMagic())`). A `+0 flaming long sword` stamped by wizard mode drops from 15 to 1 hit points on the unfixed engine and stays at 15 of 15 on the fixed one. Two controls hold on both builds: a plain long sword is melted, and a Holy Avenger keeps 38 of 35. Proved red by removing `virtual`, rebuilding and re-running: the same subject drops to 1 of 15. The same mutation turns `tools/check_virtual_override.sh` red; the stale `Weapon::isMagic` and `Armour::isMagic` lines in `tools/virtual_override.baseline` were deleted with it | no | inc-rgzr |
| Vendored libtcod 1.6.3 never recalculated its copy rectangle after a display mode change, so the console stayed clipped to the pixel size of the window before it. Three independent misses in `libtcod/src/sys_sdl2_c.c`, all reachable through public libtcod calls with no port code in the path. First, `destroy_window()` resets `scale_screen`, the sibling file-scope static the window owns, and leaves `scale_data`, so `render()` goes on copying the old rectangle after `TCOD_console_delete(NULL)` and a second `TCOD_console_init_root` -- which is what `libtcodTerm::Reset` (`src/Wlibtcod.cpp:1500`) does for both Alt-Enter and an Options-manager resolution change (`src/Managers.cpp:2358`). Second, nothing recorded the mode the window was created in: `TCOD_ctx.fullscreen` was assigned only inside the `TCOD_ANDROID` branch, so `TCOD_console_is_fullscreen()` answered false for a fullscreen window, and `create_window()` computed `TCOD_ctx.fullscreen_offsetx/y` while leaving false the flag that `actual_rendering()` needs to use them. Third, the copy box is sized from the WINDOW's shape, anchored on the console's full width, so a console whose shape does not match the window loses the difference off its top and its bottom: a 1020x768 console in a 1920x1080 window copied only rows 97..669 of 768, which is the message log and the status line (`src/TextTerm.cpp:100-125`) off the screen. It now anchors on whichever edge binds, so the surplus becomes margin at the sides; zoom is preserved and windowed rendering is untouched, because nothing changes when the shapes already agree. Note that the 4:3 shape is not forced: `OPT_FULL_RES` offers four widescreen resolutions and defaulted to index 2, 1024x768 as it shipped (`src/Tables.cpp:2410`; this fix moved the default to the last entry), which is why this is the out-of-the-box experience. None is a port artefact: both are platform-independent C in the SDL2 renderer and fail the same way on Win32, with the original typedefs, on the upstream compiler. libtcod 1.24.0 carries the second fix itself, as the same assignment in `src/libtcod/console_init.c`, and deletes `sys_sdl2_c.c` outright -- so this patch is thrown away by the upgrade on inc-1rak rather than carried forward, and both sites say so. Reported by noomenbm as Windows 11 at 1920x1080; it reproduces on macOS from this tree, because the shipped Windows binary is the mingw-w64 cross-build of these same sources | **Observed** -- `tools/check_libtcod_mode_change.sh`, which drives libtcod directly because a key script cannot reach the Alt-Enter branch (injected keys enter at `MapKey`, `src/Wlibtcod.cpp:2109`) and `@shot` saves the console surface captured before the faulty copy. Unpatched, a 1020x768 console reported `src_copy=800x600` after a windowed resize, `src_copy=800x600` in fullscreen, and `is_fullscreen=0` after a fullscreen init; patched, `src_copy=1020x768` in both and `is_fullscreen=1`, with the fullscreen destination 1063x800 inside a 1280x800 window, so the whole grid is shown with the surplus as side margin. The third miss was also observed alone: with the first two fixes in and the containment step out, the same fullscreen phase read `src_copy=1020x637`. Proved red by removing the patch and rebuilding: the same check exits 1 with all three failures named | no | inc-i2h1 |
| An illusion's declared flags were read out of its SAVE DC. `Status::Val` on an ILLUSION stati holds the save DC -- every grant site fills it from `e.saveDC` (Encounter.cpp, Effects.cpp) and `Thing::DisbeliefCheck` spends it as a DC -- while the declared flags live on the effect resource's `yval`, which `Thing::getIllusionFlags` returns. Three sites read the wrong field. `Thing::isRealTo` tested `s->Val & IL_IMPROVED`; IL_IMPROVED is 2, so it tested bit 1 of the DC and an illusion counted as improved at DC 2, 3, 6, 7, 10, 11 and so on, whatever its spell declared. An illusion the engine calls ordinary is pierced with NO saving throw by sharpened senses, scent, blindsight or tremorsense, so the ruling was inverted on half of all illusions. `Creature::PDamage` made the same mistake twice in `Creature::PDamage`: `IL_ADVANCED` is 4, so bit 2 of the DC decided whether a collapsing victim got a free disbelief check, and `IL_SPECTRAL` is 1, so bit 0 of the DC decided whether psychosomatic damage lasted 3d6 turns or 3d6 x 100. The illusory-TERRAIN path in Status.cpp already read the right field and is the model. All three now read `getIllusionFlags()`; the IL_SPECTRAL site keeps an explicit `isIllusion()` arm because a terrain illusion carries no ILLUSION stati and is spectral by design, which the old code got from `GetStatiVal`'s -1 by accident. Upstream's, not the port's: `hex/master:src/Creature.cpp:2157` carries the same line, and all three are plain integer arithmetic on two `Status` fields with no typedef, pointer or platform dependence, so Win32 with the original typedefs and compiler misbehaves identically (`src/Creature.cpp`, `src/Fight.cpp`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_illusion_flags.sh`, seed 1, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`, loading the frozen elf mage `tools/fixtures/chars/elf-mage-illusions-seed1.char`. The elf casts four illusion spells one level apart, so their save DCs rise by exactly 1 per rung while their declared flags alternate; the oracle is `Creature::PickUp` (`src/Inv.cpp`), which prints "You can't pick up an illusionary item." when the watcher pierced the illusion and "...your hand passes through it, and it winks out of existence!" when he did not. The caster is a legal watcher here because the illusory-ITEM arm of `Magic::Illusion` grants him no DISBELIEVED stati, unlike the illusory-CREATURE arm. Before the fix, rungs 1 and 2 came out INVERTED: Phantasmal Force (declares `yval: 0`, DC 15, and its own description says it is "automatically disbelieved by any creature with tremorsense, sharpened senses, scent or blindsight") was NOT pierced, and Improved Phantasmal Force (declares `IL_IMPROVED`, DC 16, description says the opposite) WAS pierced. Rungs 3 and 4 -- Spectral Force at DC 17 and Improved Spectral Force at DC 18 -- were right by arithmetic accident, which is the control: the fix does not move them. After the fix all four match their declaration. An instrumented scratch build printed the field values behind those verdicts: `s->Val=15 yval=0`, `s->Val=16 yval=2`, `s->Val=17 yval=1`, `s->Val=18 yval=3`, with `watcherSharp=1` on all four. Proved red with `--prove-red`, which restores `s->Val & IL_IMPROVED`, rebuilds and re-runs: exit 1 on rungs 1 and 2, rungs 3 and 4 still green | no | inc-pu6v.43 |
| `Creature::SavingThrow` decided every saving throw with the bare comparison `Bonus + roll >= DC`, missing SRD 3.5's rule that a natural 20 always succeeds and a natural 1 always fails regardless of the total. The comparison is plain integer arithmetic on a die roll, with no typedef, pointer or platform dependence, so Win32 with the original typedefs and compiler has the identical defect. The outcome is now computed once, right after `roll` and the final `Bonus` are both known, and used at all four sites that must agree: the message colour, the message word, the Exercise training gate and the return value -- patching only the return would have a natural 20 print `[failure]` while the engine already treated it as a success (`src/Creature.cpp`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_natural_save.sh`, seed 4, `tools/gates/Options.Dat`. `tools/keys/natural-save-runes.keys` walks a level 1 paladin (Fortitude/Will +3) into two squares of `guardian runes` (DC 27, far above his bonus) six times; one exposure prints `Will Save: 1d20 (20) +3 base = 23 vs DC 27 [failure].` on the unfixed engine and `...[success].` on the fixed one. `tools/keys/natural-save-tangle.keys` supplies volume across 14 seeds of DC 15 tanglefoot strands (safe, no damage), for a combined 142 harvested save lines, 10 natural 20s and 5 natural 1s, none in violation, on the fixed build. Proved red with `--prove-red`, which restores the bare comparison and rebuilds: the same guardian-runes line reads `[failure]` and the check exits 1. The tanglefoot volume never tested the natural-1 half, because 1 + 3 = 4 fails DC 15 with or without the rule. Since inc-b4l3g.11 the check loads the fixture `human-paladin-seed4-gate.char` and forces both cases: roll 20 against the DC 27 runes reads `[success]`, and roll 1 against `INCURSION_FORCE_SAVE_DC=1` (total 4) reads `[failure]`. Removing either half of the rule alone turns it red, and it passes on 8 sweep seeds | no | inc-e68f |
| `Resource::Event` ran only the FIRST code block on an object's handler table whose event matched, then returned, so a second block for the same trigger on the same object never ran and nothing warned. Every monster, item, spell, god and template reaches its script through this function. It is plain control flow from the import commit 7b8504a and misbehaves identically on Win32 with the original typedefs and compiler. Every matching block now runs in table order: a block that returns NOTHING passes the event on, and any other value claims it and is returned at once. An object with one block, or with message entries only, behaves as before. The compiled module held three objects with two code blocks for one event (a probe listing each resource's event entries, saved as `docs/evidence/inc-pu6v.36/evscan-probe.patch`; apply it, rebuild, and run the headless binary with `EVSCAN=1`): the will-o'-wisp's two EV_INITIALIZE blocks in `lib/mon3.irh`, and the two EV_PREREQ blocks each of Khasrach and Xavias in `lib/religion.irh`. Neither god's second block had ever run, so both were deleted and both gods keep their present behaviour; their rules are recorded in inc-pu6v.34 (`src/Annot.cpp`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_every_block_runs.sh`, seed 1, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`, driving `tools/keys/every-block-runs.keys`, which places a will-o'-wisp two cells from the player in wizard mode. Its first EV_INITIALIZE block, invisibility, ran on both builds: the wizard Stati list shows `INVIS from SS_RACE` and the map does not show its `v`. Its second block, a radius-3 light field (inc-aau6), had never run. The map row through the player and the wisp read `......@......` before the fix, 0 of 6 cells lit, and `.....,@,,,,,.` after it, 6 of 6 lit; the check is exit 1 on the unfixed build and exit 0 on the fixed one. The light probe logs a new source `S 113 110 3 255 255 255 3` on the fixed build only. Throwaway terrains in a scratch module, stepped on in one headless session on both builds: a first block that returns NOTHING let the second run, and the second saw EV_ENTER although the first had rewritten `e.Event`; on the unfixed build the second never printed. A first block that returns ABORT refused the move, one that returns DONE let it proceed, and in both the second block never ran. A message-only entry still printed. An event with a code block and a message entry, in either order, printed the code block's line and no message. Those four cases matched on both builds | no | inc-pu6v.36 |
| A character a god forsook could never rejoin it. `Character::Convert` has a rule for a forsaken character, but three gates stood in front of it: the altar menu refused him before Convert ran (`Character::PrePray`, through `isWorthyOf`), an offering at the altar brought retribution, and nothing ever cleared GS_FORSAKEN. upstream/master 7469d3e has all three; they are plain control flow, identical on Win32 with the original typedefs, so the defect is upstream's rather than the port's. On this branch `Devotion` refused him as well; that gate came with the favour curve (inc-pu6v.18) and is this design's, not upstream's. The replacement rule is this design's too: the altar menu admits a forsaken character to conversion and sacrifice, a god's offerings and devotion are rated as usual unless it declares GF_FORSAKEN_BARRED, the REJOIN_PERMILLE threshold replaces the old five-times, 5000 rule, a rejoin keeps its favour and stands at once on the rung that favour buys, and every conversion lifts the joined god's curse from its crowning gifts (`src/Prayer.cpp`) | **Traced** -- the three gates read at their source lines, and the same three in upstream/master 7469d3e. Before the fix, observed with only the altar-menu gate put back (PrePray's third `isWorthyOf` argument removed by hand, back to upstream's `isWorthyOf(e.eID,ch == 's')`, the rest of the fix left in): the forsaken character's offering at Immotian's altar was answered "Thou art not worthy of my gifts!". After it, `tools/check_forsaken_rejoin.sh` plays the repair on seed 1: the rejoin is refused at 1768 and accepted at 2524, the favour reads 2524 before and after it, he stands at once on rung 3, and the Nine Lives Stealer comes back uncursed. The whole fix was never reverted at once, so the tier stays Traced | no | inc-l4hz |
