| `TextTerm::ReadLine` wrote every typed character into `char Input[160]` (`inc/Term.h:374`) with `Input[loc++] = (char)ch` (`src/Term.cpp:3358`), never testing `loc` against the buffer's length, so the 161st character in any text prompt -- and the Enter key's terminating NUL right after it -- wrote past the end of `Input` and into whatever `TextTerm` member, or past the object, whatever heap allocation, follows it. Not confined to wizard mode: `TextTerm::StringPrompt` wraps the same routine, and ordinary play reaches it naming an item, the Journal Entry prompt, the character sheet's dump filename, and others. `git log -S "Input[loc++]" -- src/Term.cpp` returns exactly one commit, `7b8504a`, the original 2014 import; a fixed array and an unchecked monotonic index behave identically under the original typedefs and compiler, so Win32 misbehaves the same way. The write is now guarded by `loc < (int16)(sizeof(Input) - 1)`, using the buffer's own declared size rather than a repeated literal, and the character beyond that limit is silently refused -- the line already typed stays intact and the prompt stays usable -- rather than truncated at a point the caller cannot predict. `Input` is NUL-terminated after every accepted character, not only at Enter and Backspace as before, so the buffer is always a valid C string, including at the full-buffer state (`src/Term.cpp`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_readline_overflow.sh`, seed 1, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`, loading `tools/fixtures/chars/orc-barbarian-seed1-opt0822.char`. `tools/keys/readline-overflow.keys` types 161 'x' as the character sheet's Write Dump filename, one more than `Input` can hold. Content half: the longest run of 'x' in a file name under `logs/` is 161 on the unfixed build and 159 (`sizeof(Input) - 1`) on the fixed one; a second, ordinary-length dump (`stillusable.txt`) proves the prompt still works afterward on both. Structural half: the same script run under `./incursion-ubsan` (`-fsanitize=undefined`) reports `src/Term.cpp:3277: runtime error: index 160 out of bounds for type 'char[160]'` (and two more, at the following read and at the Enter-key NUL write) on the unfixed build and nothing on the fixed one. Proved red by reverting the bound check alone and rebuilding both binaries: both halves fail again, identically; restoring the fix returns both to green | no | inc-7ml0 |
| The `CHARMED` handler called `removeCreatureTarget(oCreature(s.h),TargetAny)` AFTER `MakeCompanion`, not before. `TargetAny` is `-1` (`inc/Target.h:161`), and with type `-1` `removeCreatureTarget` invalidates every entry naming the source creature (`src/Target.cpp:1149-1156`). `MakeCompanion` adds a `TargetSummoner` entry naming the player and an `OrderWalkNearMe` order naming the player (`src/Social.cpp:2749,2750-2751`), so running the wipe afterward destroyed what `MakeCompanion` had just built, and `Retarget` then dropped the invalidated entries for good (`src/Target.cpp:1440-1457`); `getLeader()` (`src/Target.cpp:993-1006`) returned NULL for every creature the player charmed, dominated or commanded. `MakeCompanion`'s own closing loop (`src/Social.cpp:2774-2782`) already repairs stale targets on every earlier companion whose `getLeader()` is the player -- it found nobody while the link was destroyed first. Moved the `removeCreatureTarget` call before the `MakeCompanion` block, leaving `Retarget` where it was. Upstream's, not the port's: plain control flow with no platform typedef and no compiler-dependent construct, so a Win32 build with the original typedefs loses the same link (`src/Status.cpp`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_command_leader_link.sh`, seed 5, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`, driving `tools/keys/command-leader-link.keys`. A wizard-mode Mage 9 dominates two summoned kobolds in turn. Unfixed: creature 1 has no "its summoner" entry naming the player after both dominations, and the two companions' Hostility dumps read one-directional -- `1 -> 2: it felt strong enmity because of personal feelings`, `2 -> 1: it felt moderate allegiance because of Kobold solidarity`. Fixed: creature 1 retains "views Volgar as its summoner", and both directions read `it felt strong allegiance because it owes fealty` -- neither regards the other as an enemy. Confirmed red on the unfixed build and green on the rebuilt one | no | inc-6ax7 |
| Erich's `GODSPEAK_LIST` (`lib/religion.irh`) carried five fully-written messages, `MSG_CUSTOM1` through `MSG_CUSTOM5`, inside a `#if 0` block, while his own script calls all five by name -- `EV_GIVE_AID` (`AID_SMITE`, goblinoids found or none found), the item-gift branch, `EV_ANGER_PULSE`, and `EV_PREREQ` (a goblinoid tries to worship him). `Character::GodMessage` (`src/Prayer.cpp:1963-1980`) only finds a custom message in the god's LIVE `GODSPEAK_LIST`, has no retry rule for `MSG_CUSTOM1..9` (only `MSG_BADSAC2..9` retries to `MSG_BADSAC1`), so every one of the five calls fell through to the engine's placeholder line, "\<Res\> says something, but you aren't sure what it is! (God messages still in progress!)". This is plain data/preprocessor content, no platform typedef or compiler-dependent construct involved, so a Win32 build with the original data and compiler shows the identical placeholder. Moved the five entries out of the `#if 0` block into the live list, byte-identical text including the "declades" misspelling in `MSG_CUSTOM5`; left `MSG_BADSAC5` and the bare placeholder constants disabled, since `MSG_BADSAC1`'s retry already gives them a sane generic line (`lib/religion.irh`) | **Traced** -- a gameplay run could not reach any of the five: `Player::ChooseGod` (`src/Create.cpp:3706-3771`) calls `isWorthyOf` under `SetSilence()` to build the "Choose a God:" menu, so a goblinoid priest never sees Erich listed and `MSG_CUSTOM5` (`EV_PREREQ`) never prints even if reached; the other four fire only from a nearby-hostile-monster prayer roll or a periodic RNG-gated event, neither reachable from a fixed key sequence without an undocumented wizard-mode monster-summon menu. `tools/check_erich_speaks.sh` extracts the live portion of Erich's `GODSPEAK_LIST` and fails if any of the five `MSG_CUSTOMn` constants his script calls is not there; confirmed red swapping in the unfixed file (all five FAIL) and green on the fix (all five PASS). `lib/program.i`, the preprocessed source the compiler consumes, carries none of the five message strings before the fix and all five, byte-identical, after rebuilding | no | inc-tek.7 |
| `struct MonMem` (`inc/Res.h`) records eleven things per player per monster kind -- Battles, Deaths, Kills, pKills, Attacks, Resists, Immune, Seen, Fought, Feats, Flags -- and nothing in the game ever wrote one of them. The storage is allocated per module (`src/Main.cpp`) and saved and loaded (`src/SaveV1.cpp`), so the save faithfully round-tripped zeros. The one place that read the record, `Monster::Describe` (`src/Help.cpp`), was shortcut past that with `/* HACKFIX */ static MonMem PerfectMem = { 255, 255, ... }` and `mm = &PerfectMem;`, the real lookup commented out beneath `//if (p->WizOpt(OPT_PERFECT_MONMEM))` -- a call to a function that exists nowhere in the tree. `Describe` reveals itself progressively on `mm->Kills`: three kills add hit dice, attack bonus and defense class, ten add the attribute sentence, fifteen widen it, twenty add the attribute numbers, twenty and fifty add skill and saving-throw numbers. With `Kills` hardwired to 255 every gate stood open, so `[R]ecall Monster` on the character sheet showed a creature's complete entry to a character who had never seen one. Three fields are now written from play -- `Seen` when the player first perceives a creature (`Player::ScrutinizeMon`, `src/Skills.cpp`), `Fought` when a blow lands between the player and a creature in either direction (`Creature::Hit`, `src/Fight.cpp`), `Kills` when the player kills one (`Creature::Death`, `src/Fight.cpp`) -- through one writer, `MonMemNote` (`src/Res.cpp`), which saturates rather than letting an 8-bit field wrap. The other eight fields are deliberately still zero. Upstream's, not the port's: the `HACKFIX` static, the commented-out branch and the absent writers are all base code, and the whole of it is struct reads and integer thresholds with no typedef, pointer or platform dependence, so Win32 on the original compiler shows the same full entry. The marker is at the read that was shortcut (`src/Help.cpp`, `src/Res.cpp`, `src/Fight.cpp`, `src/Skills.cpp`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_monster_memory.sh`, seed 4, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`. Before: an orc barbarian who had just arrived recalled the human entry and read "It has 1d8 hit points, a base attack bonus of +0, and a defense class of 10 ... naturally weak Fortitude (+0), Reflex (+0) and Will (+0). It has average strength (10), average dexterity (10), average constitution (10), average intelligence (10), average wisdom (10) and average charisma (10)." After, on the same seed and the same key script, the same box reads "It moves at 100% of normal human speed ... It has naturally weak Fortitude, Reflex and Will." and nothing else: no hit dice, no attack bonus, no defense class, no saving-throw numbers and no attribute sentence at all. After ten kills of that kind the hit dice, attack bonus, defense class and "It has average strength, average dexterity and average constitution" come back, and the saving-throw numbers correctly stay shut. The save's own `MonMem` rows, read with `tools/dump_save.sh`, separate the three writers: `kobold: Seen=1 Fought=0 Kills=0` for one only looked at, `ogre: Seen=1 Fought=1 Kills=0` for one struck once, `human: Seen=1 Fought=1 Kills=10` for ten killed, and no row at all for a kind never met. Saturation measured separately with `tools/keys/monster-memory-saturate.keys`: 309 kills landed and the row reads `Kills=255`, not 53. Each of the four sites was mutated alone, rebuilt, and watched fail | NOT sent | inc-q98a |
| Step 5's best-effort "Final, Fix-Up Tunneling" pass (`src/MakeLev.cpp:1957-2079` -- at most 26 rounds, 3 corridors per round, nearest unconnected area per region per round) sometimes leaves a portal sealed behind solid rock with no corridor reaching it: measured 9 of 156 generated levels (seeds 1-20, `tools/check_portal_reach.sh`). The pass is plain control flow with no dependence on integer width, the typedefs or the compiler, so the original Win32/MSVC build misses the same pockets. Added a door-aware connectivity walk (`passable(x,y) := FDoorAt(x,y) ? true : !SolidAt(x,y)`, the same rule `Map::FloodConnectA` already uses) and a repair that runs at the end of `Map::Generate`: for each portal outside the component holding the most portals, force its own square to floor if solid (mirroring the existing up-stairs pocket carve at `src/MakeLev.cpp:1944`), then a four-way breadth-first search finds the nearest square of that component over ground that is already open or that `WriteAt` will accept a floor write on (`At(x,y).Priority <= PRIO_ROOM_FLOOR`, excluding the border ring `WriteAt` never writes below `PRIO_MAX`), and carves exactly that path -- floor and corridor walls, skipping any neighbour that is already passable, at `Con[CORRIDOR_REGION]`, `PRIO_ROOM_FLOOR` and `PRIO_CORRIDOR_WALL`, the same two priorities `Map::Tunnel`'s own `WriteCorridor` block uses -- and never with `Force=true`. Because every square on the returned path was verified writable before anything was written, the carve cannot be refused partway; only a genuine no-path case (a vault or the like standing in every route) falls through to regeneration. `Map::PortalsConnected()` re-checks the same walk after generation; `Game::GetDungeonMap` (`src/Feature.cpp`) discards and regenerates a level that still fails it, bounded at 10 attempts, and accepts the level with a logged `Error()` on the tenth (`src/MakeLev.cpp`). An earlier version of this fix carved a blind L-shaped path with no writability check, which a square of higher priority (room furniture, a river streamer, a vault) could and did stop dead partway -- measured on 13 of the 14 repair attempts in one 20-seed sweep -- turning "dig, regenerate only on failure" into "regenerate almost always"; replacing the L with the search fixed it (`src/MakeLev.cpp`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_portal_reach.sh`, seeds 1-20, `tools/gates/Options.Dat`, `tools/keys/dive.keys`. Before the fix: 9 of 156 generated levels stranded a portal. After: 0 stranded across the same 20-seed sweep (157 kept levels, 19 of 20 seeds measured), and every one of the 9 stranded portals -- including both solid-own-square cases, `seed 11 depth 7` and `seed 13 depth 4` -- was repaired by the corridor search alone; 0 regenerations were needed. Probe on/off produces byte-identical screens on the same seed (seed 3, which needs a repair), confirming the repair runs unconditionally rather than only when measured. The regeneration retry in `src/Feature.cpp` -- `delete m` on a fully generated Map, untested by the 20-seed sweep above -- is exercised on demand with the test hook `INCURSION_PORTAL_REPAIR_OFF` (`carve` disables only the corridor search, so the retry fires for real; `all` disables both, reproducing the unfixed defect): with `=carve` on the eight seeds that ever stranded a portal, 14 regenerations fired, the highest of any level being its 3rd attempt, every level ended connected, and no run added a line to its `logs/errors.log`. `tools/check_portal_reach.sh --prove-red` runs with `=all` and PASSES only when the check FAILS -- proof this guard can be shown red, not only green -- and reproduces the original 9-of-156 defect on today's binary. Gameplay evidence, seed 3 depth 4, `tools/dump_save.sh` on a save taken with and without `INCURSION_PORTAL_REPAIR_OFF=all`: before, the up staircase at (5,91) has all eight neighbours solid and reads "no open path from the player"; after, one neighbour is open and it reads reachable in 40 steps (`docs/evidence/inc-5caj/`, untracked) | no | inc-5caj |
| `Magic::ABallBeamBolt`'s line walk (`src/Magic.cpp:2145-2171` before this fix) added the first creature it crossed to the target list and stopped -- `if (cr != e.EVictim) { ADD_TARGET(cr); } if (!isMulti) goto OuterBreak;` -- so a bolt or ray never reached the creature the caster chose if any other body stood between them. Most bolts carry no `EF_ATTACK`, so no attack roll ever ran to redirect the shot; `DoHits` then applied the effect to the intervening body with no friend filter, so an ally, a summon or an animated zombie absorbed a spell aimed at the foe behind it. This closes `inc-c4l4` (Magic Missile) and is the companion defect to the arrow-walk fix already in this table for `Creature::RAttack`. The walk depends on no integer width, typedef or platform: it behaves identically on Win32 with the original types. The walk now passes every intervening body and stops only at the chosen target's own square; for an `EF_ATTACK` effect, `Magic::MagicStrike`'s kludge branch (`src/Magic.cpp:1095`) now rolls once against the touch defence (see the touch-defence row above) plus a cover penalty of 4 per occupied square, computed by reusing `Magic::PredictVictimsOfBallBeamBolt`'s own walk rather than a third copy of the line-walk geometry; on a miss, the same band arithmetic phase 2 gave `Creature::RAttack` picks which recorded square's head creature took the shot instead, without a second roll. An effect with no `EF_ATTACK` still passes every body and reaches the chosen target unerringly. A beam or chain is unchanged: it strikes everything in the line by design (`src/Magic.cpp`, `inc/Events.h`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_line_of_fire_spell.sh`, seed 1, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`. `INCURSION_LOF_SPELL_PROBE=1` arms `LOFSpellProbe`, which places four giant rats in a line -- "near" and "far" each alone in their own square, "cover" and "target" sharing the square at distance 4 with "cover" as that square's head -- and casts under a forced d20 roll (`LOFSetForcedRoll`), reading back which rat lost hit points. Ten cases: both boundaries of each of the three cover bands, a natural 20, a roll below the bare touch defence, an unerring Magic Missile cast over the same three bodies, and a Lightning Bolt beam. Unfixed (the walk's fix alone reverted): `pass=3 fail=7`, and critically the unerring case reads `near_hp=9984 far_hp=10000 cover_hp=10000 target_hp=10000` -- the ally standing nearest the shooter took the hit and the chosen target took nothing, the exact defect this row describes. Fixed: `pass=10 fail=0`, the unerring case reads `near_hp=10000 far_hp=10000 cover_hp=10000 target_hp=9983` -- every intervening body unharmed and only the chosen target damaged -- and the beam case still damages all four (`near_hp=9962 far_hp=9984 cover_hp=9967 target_hp=9985`), confirming that case is unaffected by the fix | no | inc-30ps.3 |
| Player mana regen only ticks while `cMana()` is at or above a floor percentage of `nhMana()`; the base code read that floor as `min(35+SkillLevel(SK_CONCENT)*2,80)`, so a player with no Concentration regenerates from 35% up and training the skill RAISES the floor toward 80%, punishing practice. The intended shape is the reverse: `max(80-SkillLevel(SK_CONCENT)*2,35)` -- the floor starts at 80% and FALLS toward 35% as Concentration rises. The identical line stands at hex/master `src/Creature.cpp:1534`, plain integer arithmetic with no platform typedef, so a Win32 0.6.9 build misbehaves the same way. Added `Creature::ManaRegenFloor()` (`inc/Creature.h`) computing the corrected formula; `Creature::DoTurn` now calls it and the ManaPulse curve inside the gated block is unchanged (`src/Creature.cpp`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_mana_regen_floor.sh`, seed 4, `tools/gates/Options.Dat`, loading `tools/fixtures/chars/orc-mage-seed4-gate.char`. `INCURSION_MANA_FLOOR_PROBE=1` arms `Character::ManaFloorProbe()`, which zeroes held mana, sets spent mana so current mana is 40% of the pool, forces `SkillLevel(SK_CONCENT)` low (0 raw ranks) then high (30 raw ranks), clears `ManaPulse`, and drives 50 real ticks through `Creature::DoTurn()`. Fixed: low case `skill=2 before=28 after=28` (blocked, floor 80%), high case `skill=32 before=28 after=32` (regen ran, floor 35%). `--prove-red`, reverting only the condition to the base-code formula and rebuilding: low case `skill=2 before=28 after=32` (regen ran when it should have been blocked) and high case `skill=32 before=28 after=28` (blocked when it should have run) -- the exact inversion, PROVED RED, then restored and re-measured green. A second check reaches the same property through ordinary play with no forced state: `tools/check_mana_regen_cast.sh`, same seed and fixture, drives `tools/keys/mana-regen-cast.keys`, which casts Burning Hands (a spell with no lasting hold on mana) 35 times aimed away from the caster into open floor, then searches three times (about 75 turns) without resting. Fixed build: mana went 72/72 -> 43/72 (59%, inside the 35-80% band the fix cares about) after the casts, and stayed at 43/72 after the wait, held mana 0 (confirmed by `tools/dump_save.sh` on the session's own save). `--prove-red` on the same script: mana went 72/72 -> 44/72 (61%) after the casts, then rose to 52/72 during the wait -- the un-fixed floor letting regen run where the fixed one blocks it -- PROVED RED, then restored and re-measured green | no | inc-41kg |
| A spell with `EF_LOSEMANA` holds its cost (`hMana`) and nothing lowers `hMana` when the buff ends, and a holding spell cast again while its buff stands holds its cost again, so a dropped or recast buff leaves held mana that does not regenerate until rest. The `LoseMana(cost, EF_LOSEMANA)` call sites are identical in hex/master and use plain integer arithmetic, so a Win32 0.6.9 build misbehaves the same way. Added `Creature::SpendSpellMana` (a second casting while a hold stands spends instead of holding) and `ReleaseHeldMana`, called by the shared stati-removal macro (the removal macro), which moves a released hold from `hMana` to `uMana` (`src/Status.cpp`); a `HELD_MANA` stati on the caster records each hold | **Observed** -- `tools/check_held_mana.sh`, seed 4, `tools/gates/Options.Dat`, loading `tools/fixtures/chars/human-priest-mage-seed4-gate.char`: dropping Shield (cost 5) from the Cancel menu moves held 5 -> 0 and spent 0 -> 5, and a recast holds 5, not 10. `--prove-red`, making `ReleaseHeldMana` return at once: held stays 5 (FAIL 1a) | no | inc-w6n5 |
| `Creature::Mount` (`src/Skills.cpp`) and the `MountChecks:` block of `Character::CalcValues` (`src/Values.cpp`) tested a mount's size by different rules. `Mount` refused a mount no larger than its rider, except Small on Small and a dwarf on a Medium mount, and refused a quadruped animal more than one size larger. `CalcValues` threw the rider when the mount was no larger, with a Small exception that compared the mount's size with itself (a typo, so it never excluded anything correctly), and also threw for an animal more than one size larger. A size change could therefore throw a rider the mount test had admitted, or the mount test could refuse what recalculation kept. Owner's ruling (2026-10-26): a mount MUST be at least one size category larger than its rider, by current `GetAttr(A_SIZ)`, with no upper limit and no exception. Both tests now apply only that rule; the two exception clauses and both upper-limit branches are deleted. Plain integer comparisons and type checks, so the defect is the same on Win32 and under the original compiler, and not a port artifact | **Observed** -- `tools/check_mount_size.sh` (fixture `tools/fixtures/chars/human-paladin-seed4-opt0822.char`, settings `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`), red before on cases 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6, green after on all six. Before (outcome): case 1 small->tiny rider, tiger: thrown; case 2 small rider, elephant: refused ("too large for you to ride effectively"); case 3 small rider, small pony: admitted; case 4 medium rider, medium pony: admitted; case 5 medium->large rider, tiger: thrown; case 6 medium->small rider, tiger: thrown. After: case 1 stays; case 2 admitted, unchanged; case 3 refused; case 4 refused; case 5 thrown; case 6 stays. Reverting only the `Skills.cpp` change fails cases 2, 3, 4; reverting only the `Values.cpp` change fails cases 1, 6 | no | inc-2mea |
| `Character::Convert`'s join branch reset the god being joined unconditionally and the reset was itself PARTIAL: `for (i=0;i!=10;i++) SacVals[e.godNum][i] = 0;` zeroed only ten of the twelve `SacVals` slots, so a value set through the "stack" slot (index `MAX_SAC_CATS+1`, the one `gainFavour(...,stack=true)` and the wizard "Set Favour" command both write) survived a join by accident while sacrifice favour did not, and the character's rank never rose to meet it because the very next block called `gainedFavour` only `if (rejoin)`. `upstream/master` 7469d3e has the same twelve slots and the same ten-slot loop at its `src/Prayer.cpp:1221`; it is plain control flow, identical on Win32 with the original typedefs. The keep-by-default behaviour and the `GF_CONVERT_RESETS` opt-out are this project's design ruling and are NOT claimed as an upstream fix: the reset now runs only when the joined god declares that flag, and then clears all twelve slots (`MAX_SAC_CATS+2`); `gainedFavour` now runs unconditionally so an ordinary join stands at once on the rung its kept favour buys (`src/Prayer.cpp`, `inc/Defines.h`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_convert_keeps_favour.sh`, seed 1, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`, driving `tools/keys/convert-keeps-favour.keys` against a scratch module carrying the test god `tools/fixtures/convert-test-god.irh` ("Jointest"). Wizard "Set Favour" puts 90000 on Jointest while the character follows no one, then a real altar join is played through `Character::Convert`. Proved RED against the unmodified source on 2026-09-20: both the "keep" and "reset" module variants read `Jointest   Lev 0, Fav 90000` after the join (wanted `Lev 9, Fav 90000` for keep, `Lev 0, Fav     0` for reset) -- the favour survived by the accident described above but bought no rank, and a god declaring the (shimmed) flag could not reset the one slot the old loop never reached either. GREEN, same seed, same options, same key script, on the rebuilt binary: the keep variant reads `Jointest   Lev 9, Fav 90000` and the reset variant reads `Jointest   Lev 0, Fav     0`, so the join keeps every slot and stands at once on the uncapped rung, and the flag starts a stranger at zero. THE ABI STEP THIS NEEDED, because `GF_CONVERT_RESETS` advanced `GF_LAST` from 31 to 32: `TGod::Flags` was sized `(GF_LAST/8)+1` (now fixed, `inc/Res.h:638`), so it grew a byte, `sizeof(TGod)` grew from 132 to 136, and the pre-existing `static_assert(sizeof(TGod) == 132, ...)` (now 136, `src/AbiCheck.cpp:97`) stopped the build, which is exactly what that guard is for. `sizeof(TGod)` is the module's god-blob stride (`src/RComp.cpp:480`, `src/SaveV1.cpp:865`), so the assertion was not relaxed casually. The array is now a FIXED 8 bytes, room for 64 flags, which costs nothing because 133 rounds up to 136 anyway, so adding a god flag no longer moves the struct at all; the size assertion carries the measured 136, and a new `static_assert(GF_LAST <= 8 * sizeof(((TGod*)0)->Flags), ...)` beside it makes outgrowing the array a build error rather than a silent stride change. NO SAVE MIGRATION FOLLOWS, and this was verified rather than assumed: `inc/Res.h:823` records that no check ever produces a `T_MODULE` v1 record, because `Game::SaveModule` stays raw and a player's save writes `MainRegistry` group 0, which holds no `Module`, so a save file carries no `TGod`; only the module file's stride moved, and every build recompiles `mod/Incursion.Mod` beside the binary. Confirmed empirically on the rebuilt binary: `tools/check_schema_roundtrip.sh` PASS ("v1 round trip ... is exact"), `tools/check_char_fixture.sh` PASS -- a fixture frozen before this change still loads as Harmonious Fox, Lizardfolk Monk 1, STR 14/00, and comes back byte-identical -- `tools/check_abi.sh` PASS and `tools/check_module_rebuild.sh` PASS. The eleven other resource types that size a flag array the same way and have no size assertion at all are filed as inc-sjv4 | no | inc-62c7 |
| `Creature::getTroubles` computed `man`, the mana-trouble signal fed to Character::Pray, as the PERCENTAGE OF MANA STILL HELD rather than the percentage missing, then ran it through a ladder written for a deficit ("92 <= man is P_CRITICAL"): a caster at full mana read as critically low on mana, and a caster who had run dry read as having no mana trouble at all. The caster-relevance line right after it (`man = ((man*30L) + (man*70L*CasterLev())/max(1,ChallengeRating()))/100`) confirms the intended meaning -- it SHRINKS the value for a non-caster, which only makes sense as "less trouble for whoever cares least about mana", not as a shrunk remaining-mana figure. A second, paired gap: TROUBLE_FATIGUE (`inc/Defines.h:4004`) is paired to AID_REFRESH in AidPairs (`src/Prayer.cpp:1306`) but `getTroubles` never assigned it, so no amount of fatigue could ever ask a god to remove it, though seven gods' Desc text promises exactly that. Both are plain integer arithmetic and a plain omitted assignment, with no typedef, pointer width, endianness or compiler extension involved, so a Win32 build with the original typedefs misreads mana and ignores fatigue identically. Rewrote `man` as the deficit, mirroring `inj` two lines above it and floored at 0, and added a TROUBLE_FATIGUE ladder using the same deficit shape against `GetAttr(A_FAT)`/`cFP`, topping out at P_URGENT rather than injury's P_CRITICAL because exhaustion does not kill (`src/Prayer.cpp`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_pray_troubles.sh --prove-red`, seed 5, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`, driving `tools/keys/pray-troubles.keys`. A fresh Divine Champion of Hesani is drained of mana (199 -> 0) and fatigue (4 -> 1 of 4) by `tools/keys/drain-mana.keys`, then prays: the fixed build prints "Your magical energy is replenished!" and "You no longer feel fatigued." once each, beside the unconditional MSG_PRAYER control. Reverting only the `man` line and rebuilding drops the mana-aid line while the fatigue-aid line and the control both survive -- the mutation the check declares touches only HALF 1, so `tools/check_pray_fatigue.sh` below is what proves HALF 2. THE DUPLICATE-AID REMOVAL: this same drained-of-both character used to match AID_REFRESH twice on one prayer -- TROUBLE_LOWMANA and TROUBLE_FATIGUE both paired to it in AidPairs (`src/Prayer.cpp:1330`, now deleted) -- and print "You no longer feel fatigued." twice, though it cost no extra favour (`FavPenalty` is charged once, after the loop). Before this bead's fatigue ladder existed, that LOWMANA row was the ONLY way AID_REFRESH could ever fire, so once the ladder made TROUBLE_FATIGUE real the row became redundant; deleted it and shrank `AidPairs`'s declared size from 20 to match. Guarded by a new library assertion, `check_expect_count` (`tools/check_lib.sh`, with its own `--selftest` cases), which counts occurrences instead of testing presence -- `check_expect` reports "1/1 screens" whether a string appears once or twice on the one screen these checks read, so it could not see this duplicate at all. `tools/check_pray_troubles.sh` now asserts "longer feel fatigued" appears EXACTLY ONCE, and carries a second `check_mutation` that restores the deleted row (and its array-size bump); `check_lib.sh`'s `--prove-red` only exercises the FIRST declared mutation, so this one was proved by moving it to the front (same technique `tools/check_school_focus_menu.sh` already uses for its own second mutation): `FAIL 2 occurrence(s), wanted 1, across 1 screens: longer feel fatigued`, then moved back. The repair also exposed that `tools/check_pray_aid_int32.sh` (inc-pf0p) had been passing BECAUSE of this inversion -- its own header said so -- so that check's key script now `@include`s the same drain before it prays; confirmed still red and green on ITS OWN mutation (the int16 `cFavour` cast) afterward. `tools/check_pray_fatigue.sh --prove-red`, same seed and options, drives `tools/keys/pray-fatigue.keys`, which drains ONLY fatigue (`tools/keys/drain-fatigue.keys`) and leaves mana full: `man` then computes to 0, so `TROUBLE_LOWMANA` cannot fire and "You no longer feel fatigued." can only come from the `TROUBLE_FATIGUE` ladder. It also asserts "Your magical energy is replenished!" is ABSENT, which is the other half of HALF 1 that `check_pray_troubles.sh` cannot see: a genuinely full-mana caster must carry no mana trouble at all, where before the fix he carried one for free. The check's mutation had to disable all three rungs of the ladder together (`0 && ` on each of the three `if`/`else if` guards), not just the top one: mutating only `if (70 <= fat)` stayed green, because this character's 75 percent deficit still satisfied the `else if (50 <= fat)` rung directly behind it and TROUBLE_FATIGUE fired at P_MODERATE instead. With all three disabled the fixed build still passes (Mana:199/199, FP:1/4 at prayer time) and the mutated build drops the fatigue line while the (absent) mana line and the control are unaffected -- confirmed both ways. THE MONSTER-AI HALF, OBSERVED IN GAMEPLAY (`tools/check_monster_mana_trouble.sh --prove-red`), because `getTroubles` runs for every creature, not just the player, and `Creature::worstTrouble` (`src/Prayer.cpp:836`) feeds `src/Monster.cpp:586`'s per-turn action list -- this is also why the `TROUBLE_FATIGUE` ladder above had to move inside an `if (isPlayer())` guard (matching the existing `TROUBLE_NOBOOK`/`TROUBLE_ENEMIES` precedent in the same function): an unguarded monster fatigue trouble drifted two live merge-gate checks, `tools/check_dungeonmap_bounds.sh` and `tools/check_god_pulse_request.sh`, because a fatigued monster got a spurious extra action candidate it never had before. The `man` mutation this check guards is not player-scoped and was never the drift's cause. SECOND ATTEMPT, because the first read a contaminated instrument: "Examine Nearby Things" calls `Creature::Dump` -> `CalcValues()`, and `Monster::CalcValues` re-rolls `mMana` on every call, so repeated inspection moved the very number being read (the 127-vs-84 figures once quoted here are deleted); and "the potion is missing from a later inventory dump" is a residue with other explanations, not an observed act. The corrected method inspects the monster EXACTLY ONCE, before it ever takes a turn, and reads the MESSAGE the game prints at the moment of drinking off the plain screen thereafter, with no further inspection: "The kobold mage drinks a ? smoky black potion." (`src/Magic.cpp:3632`). A third finding explained why the first attempt's full-mana arm looked inert on either build: `src/Monster.cpp:586` only ADDS the trouble-fixing act to the candidate list, and the monster executes its highest-priority entry -- a hostile kobold in melee always has a combat act that outranks it. Removed the competition instead of outlasting it: wizard "Make Player Master of Monster" (`Monster::MakeCompanion`) turns the same mage-templated kobold (lib/mon2.irh:4575, guarantees `1d1 $"potion" of $"mana"`) into the player's companion, so its own AI still runs but has nothing better to do. One pair, full mana (144/144, quoted and identical in both arms), five turns, no inspection: fixed build prints no drink message at all; unfixed build (`man` reverted) drinks on turn 1 of 5, turn 198016, the message quoted above. Reproduced twice byte-identical on the fixed build. THE POTION PINNED BY ELIMINATION, NOT ARGUMENT: the kobold carries three potions and the drink message names only an unidentified flavour, so a THIRD gap remained -- nothing showed which potion was drunk, only reasoning (full HP so no injury trouble, Growth answers no EP_FIX_TROUBLE purpose). Closed with a second and FINAL inventory read, taken once, after all five measured turns are over -- not a repeat of the CalcValues() fault, because the decision under measurement has already happened by that point. Fixed build's final inventory still lists all three potions; the unfixed build's lists only Healing and Growth. Mana is the one potion missing, on the one build that drank something, confirming which potion by elimination. The empty-mana direction was not pursued further -- the first attempt's finding that this kobold's pool cannot be driven below roughly a third by combat, Dispel Magic or Magic Missile stands and was not re-tested. Evidence and full route notes in `docs/evidence/inc-pu6v.40/README.md` (untracked) | no | inc-pu6v.40 |
