| Companion ("PHD") pools charged their followers on a different scale from their own limit, and a full pool never charged its deficit to the party pool. `Player::GetGroupXCR` charged each follower `max(10,(j+2)^3)` while the limit is `XCR(MaxGroupCR)` = `(CR+3)^3`, and `(j+2)^3 == XCR(j-1)`, so every follower counted one CR short; `Monster::MakeCompanion` computed its admission test by rounding through `XCRtoCR`, and `XCRtoCR` of a zero or negative remainder returns `-8`, whose `XCR` is `+10`, so a creature's pool deficit collapsed to a nominal `+10` and was never charged against `PHD_PARTY`. Now `GetGroupXCR` charges `XCR(j)` and `XCR(AddCR)`, and `MakeCompanion` tests raw XCR units: a creature is admitted iff its own pool holds it, or, for a non-party pool, the party pool (minus that pool's own overflow, already counted in `GetGroupXCR(PHD_PARTY)`) holds the deficit. Upstream's, not the port's: both are plain integer arithmetic on the same fields with no typedef, pointer or compiler dependence, so a Win32 0.6.9 build under- or over-charges identically (`src/Social.cpp`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_follower_cap.sh`, seed 1, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`, loading the frozen `tools/fixtures/chars/orc-mage-seed1-opt0822.char` (level 1, PHD_MAGIC max CR 1, PHD_PARTY max CR 3). Reading the wizard "Group CR Totals" block of the 8/8 Examine dump. Fault 1: a dominated CR-0 kobold costs `XCR(0)=55`, so `PHD MAGIC` reads `0 / 1` fixed against `-8 / 1` upstream. Fault 2: a summoned hill giant (CR 7, cost `XCR(7)=1000`) is dominated but then refused -- "The hill giant breaks free of your control!" -- leaving both pools at the control reading, `PHD MAGIC 0 / 1` and `PHD PARTY -8 / 3`, where upstream admitted the giant (`PHD MAGIC 8 / 1`, `PHD PARTY 7 / 3`). Proved red both by the check's declared `--prove-red` mutation and by a manual revert of both sites; see the bead note | no | inc-omm1 |
