| Undeath to Death carried `EF_DEATH` in its `Flags:` line alongside `tval: MA_UNDEAD`, so `Magic::isTarget` (`src/Magic.cpp:441-443`) refused the spell against every legal target: it treats `EF_DEATH` as a death effect that any creature whose `ResistLevel(AD_NECR) == -1` ignores, and every undead is necromancy-immune through `UNDEAD_IMMUNITIES` (`lib/defines.irh:7`), so the only creatures the spell could affect were the ones it always refused. Removed only `EF_DEATH`, keeping `EF_LIM_MTYPE`; the `EV_MAGIC_HIT` handler, aval/lval/qval/pval and the Desc are unchanged. The same data ships on Win32 with the original compiler and misbehaves identically, so it is not a port artefact (`lib/pspells.irh`) | **Observed** -- `tools/keys/undeath-to-death-area.keys`, seeds 1-10, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`. The target prompt for the aimed undead read `[Invalid Target]` on all 10 before-runs with the turn counter unchanged (a refused cast, seed 1 log `logs/inc-kgzx/before/seed-1/logs/screens/0006-target-prompt.txt`); after, the prompt accepts the target and the cast resolves against the summoned undead. After-runs in `logs/inc-kgzx/after-flag/seed-N` | no | inc-kgzx |
| `Creature::GainInherentBonus` refused a tome read at the inherent cap only when the total was EXACTLY the cap (`if (CurrBonus == MaxBonus)`); a total already ABOVE the cap fell through, printed a false "you feel <attr>" gain and mutated the bonus. Reachable in play: the shambling mound's electricity handler grants an uncapped `ADJUST_INH` A_CON +1d4 (`lib/mon3.irh:2112`) on top of a tome-filled cap, and a player in mound form reads a tome. Plain control flow on two integer fields, no typedef, pointer-width or compiler dependence, so Win32 with the original typedefs and compiler misbehaves identically; changed only `==` to `>=` (`src/Creature.cpp`) | **Observed** -- played, not probed (the play check was retired in inc-b4l3g.9 and lives in history at commit a05fefd1; `tools/check_inh_wasteful.sh` is the standing guard), seed 1, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-18.dat`. An orc barbarian fills CON's inherent bonus to the cap with a +5 Libram of Bodily Health, polymorphs (wizard mode) into a shambling mound, takes a summoned grid bug's electric bite ("Grid Bug's Attack: 1d20 (18) +2 = 20 vs. Def 11 [hit]" / "Grid Bug's Damage: 1d3 = 1 Lightning"), and reads a second Libram through the ordinary `r` command. Before the fix (`==` restored by hand, rebuilt, reverted after): "You feel hardier. The tome vanishes!" -- a false gain. After: "You feel a profound sense of wastefulness. The tome vanishes!", CON unchanged on the sheet both sides. The original probe evidence (`tools/check_inh_wasteful.sh`, `INCURSION_INH_PROBE=1`: `inh: at=0 max=5 curr=6 message=gain after=6` before, `message=wasteful` after) still runs and still passes | no | inc-bsqm |
| Intensive Study ("Improve Caster Level") raised the caster level but granted no spell slot. `Player::GainAbility`'s `case CA_SPELLCASTING` raised `SpellSlots[i]` up to `SpellTable[Abilities[ab]][i]` -- the RAW class level -- while the study raises `AbilityLevel(CA_SPELLCASTING)` through `IntStudy[STUDY_CASTING]` (`Creature::AbilityLevel`), and nothing read the effective level, so the slots never followed the caster level. The game's own text promises otherwise: `lib/help.irh` says caster levels determine the number of spell slots and its worked example has Lady Sarah's third Intensive Study "give her a fourth-level spell slot", and the feat description (`src/FeatTab.cpp:507`) repeats "gives you access to the higher level spells on the spellcasting chart". One `Character::RaiseSpellSlotsToChart()` now raises each `SpellSlots[i]` (never lowering) to `SpellTable[AbilityLevel(CA_SPELLCASTING)][i]`, guarded to the table's 27 rows (0..26); `GainAbility` calls it for `CA_SPELLCASTING` and `GainFeat` calls it after `IntStudy[choice]++` when the study is `STUDY_CASTING`. Upstream's, not the port's: plain table indexing and integer arithmetic with no typedef, pointer-width or compiler dependence, so a Win32 0.6.9 build grants the same nothing (`src/Create.cpp`) | **Observed** -- `tools/check_study_slots.sh`, seed 1, `tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat`, loading the frozen Bard caster `tools/fixtures/chars/prestige-geomancy-seed1-opt0822.char`. The probe applies the study exactly as `GainFeat` now does: before, `STUDY_PROBE raw=3 eff=4 slots=2,1,... effchart=3,2,...` and the check exits 1; after, `slots=3,2,...` equals `effchart` and it exits 0. Proved red by reverting the fix in `GainFeat`/`GainAbility`. The no-study control `INCURSION_LEVELUP_PROBE` grants one ordinary level through `GainAbility(CA_SPELLCASTING)` and reads `LEVELUP_PROBE raw=2 slots=2,0,... chart=2,0,...`, so an ordinary level-up still equals the chart. Save compatibility: the same two fixtures (this caster and the non-caster `tools/fixtures/chars/orc-barbarian-seed1-opt0822.char`) load through `tools/keys/load-char-sheet.keys`, and their sheet bodies are byte-identical to the fixture sheets; the caster keeps `Spell Slots 2+5 (6) / 1+1 (2)` and the barbarian shows no spell-slot line. Gameplay, not a probe: `tools/check_intensive_study_play.sh` drives `tools/keys/intensive-study-caster-sheet.keys` on the same fixture, leveling Bard 7 to Bard 9 through the sheet's ordinary `G` command and taking Intensive Study's caster-level study through the real level-9 feat menu. Before (the study-site call reverted): `Spell Slots    5th  3+5 (7) / 2+2 (4)`. After: `Spell Slots    5th  3+5 (7) / 2+2 (4) / 1+1 (2)`. Proved red with `tools/check_intensive_study_play.sh --prove-red` | no | inc-ngku |
| `Player::ChooseGod` ended with an unconditional `setGodFlags(GodID,GS_INVOLVED)` after `if (i >= 0) GodID = Candidates[i];`. A declined menu (`i == -1`, which `MO_RECREATE` allows) therefore called `setGodFlags(0,...)`; `GodNum(0)` is -1, so the call wrote `godFlags[-1]`. Gate the call on a real pick. Upstream's, not the port's: the line is in the v0.6.5B source (7b8504a, the import commit) as plain member indexing with no typedef, pointer width, endianness or compiler extension in it, so the original Win32/MSVC build writes the same out-of-bounds slot (`src/Create.cpp`) | **Traced** -- read the base source `7b8504a:src/Create.cpp` end to end: the two statements are consecutive with no pick test around the second | no | inc-z8qn |
| One `DefaultMessages` row in `Character::GodMessage` carried an unbalanced speech mark: `MSG_OUT_OF_AID` opened a `|` before "Thou must journey on thine own now." and never closed it, and the renderer treats each `|` as a speech mark that toggles the god's voice colour and prints `"`, so the quote stayed open and the colour was never restored. Added the closing `|`; no wording changed. The text is Julian Mensch's v0.6.5B source (`7b8504a`) and the renderer behaves identically on Win32 with the original typedefs and compiler, so it is upstream's rather than the port's (`src/Prayer.cpp`) | **Traced** -- `tools/check_god_speech_marks.sh` counts string literals per `DefaultMessages` entry (adjacent literals concatenate) and exits 1 naming `MSG_OUT_OF_AID` on the unfixed file; the fix makes it exit 0, and a second mutation removing the closing `|` of another row fails again. No behavioural before/after run | no | inc-pu6v.66 |
| The AID_BERSERK message read "you fly into a berzerk rage!", misspelling "berserk", in both the `AID_BERSERK` case of `Character::GiveAid` and the matching `MSG_AID + AID_BERSERK` `DefaultMessages` row. Corrected the spelling in both; other instances in `src/Status.cpp` and `lib/` are left for inc-jv20. The text is Julian Mensch's v0.6.5B source and reads identically on Win32, so it is upstream's (`src/Prayer.cpp`) | **Traced** -- `grep -n -i berzerk src/Prayer.cpp` returns nothing after the change; a spelling slip in a static string with no platform dependence. No behavioural before/after run | no | inc-pu6v.66 |
